  "summary": "Popular narrative poetry tradition illustrated with woodcut prints, sold as cheap pamphlets at fairs and markets.",
  "description": "Characterized by simple woodcut illustrations with bold black outlines and minimal detail, reflecting the limited resources of the printers and the need for quick production. Developed as an oral tradition that transitioned to print, serving both entertainment and education in rural communities.",
  "culturalContext": "Cordel literature forms one of the least altered continuations of the Western traditions of popular literature, such as chapbooks, and popular prints. Its history dates back to the 16th century, when the printing of oral reports became popularized in the Renaissance. This genre derives from the papel volante tradition of Portugal, a literary genre also found in Spain during the 18th and 19th centuries, and offered readers a wide array of topics, from basic instruction to political tracts. The name comes from the way the leaflets were traditionally displayed for sale, hung on ropes, twine or string in Portugal. It remains a popular literary form in Brazil. In the Northeastern region of Brazil, the name was adopted, but the leaflet may or may not be displayed on string. Some poems are illustrated with woodcuts, also used on the covers. The most common stanzas are those of ten, eight or six verses. The authors, or cordelistas, recite these verses in a melodious and cadenced way, accompanied by a musical instrument named viola. Readings or declamations of these verses are performed to…",
